faqs:
  - q: "What exactly does the law require in my rental?"
    a: "Working smoke alarms within 3 metres of every bedroom doorway, at least one on each level of the property, and any new or replacement alarms must be long-life photoelectric or hardwired. The landlord must have working alarms in place at the start of every tenancy; tenants must replace batteries (in older replaceable-battery alarms) during the tenancy and must not disable alarms. Caravans, sleepouts and self-contained units count too."
  - q: "What happens if a rental doesn't comply?"
    a: "Failing to meet smoke alarm obligations is an unlawful act under the Residential Tenancies Act, and the Tenancy Tribunal can award exemplary damages of up to $7,200. Insurance is the quieter risk — after a fire, one of the first questions is whether the alarms met the rules. A $99 check per property is cheap next to either conversation."
  - q: "Do you work with property managers on multiple rentals?"
    a: "All the time — portfolio checks across Riccarton, Ilam, Hornby and the wider city are a big part of our week. We schedule directly with tenants, handle access politely, and send each property's report to your office the same day. Ask about portfolio rates when you call."
  - q: "How often should rental alarms be checked?"
    a: "The practical rhythm: a check at every tenancy changeover (that's when the 'working alarms at the start of the tenancy' obligation bites), plus an annual once-over if tenancies run long. Many of our landlord clients simply book us for the gap between tenants — the report then sits in the file as proof the obligation was met on day one."
